### Example Docs

`aiDoc/examples/` is the explanatory example layer: it tells AI the standard for organizing and writing each layer. Examples are not meant to be copied verbatim; read the corresponding example before adding a file to a layer. When real code and an example disagree, the real code wins — update the example.

### Doc Maintenance

High-level rules live in `AGENTS.md`; details live in `aiDoc/`. This file keeps only coarse "task family → aiDoc area" pointers; the detailed "task → must-read docs" routing table is maintained solely in `aiDoc/README.md` — never list the full inventory here, or the two copies drift. When aiDoc child docs are added or removed, the index and routing table in `aiDoc/README.md` must be updated in sync.

### Code-Reading Constraints

Never read dependency or build-output directories such as `node_modules/`, `.venv/`, `__pycache__/`, `vendor/`, `dist/`, `build/`. Never read `.env`, private keys, or other sensitive files.

## Operating Invariants

Every AI working in this repository must always follow:

1. **One fact, one maintained home**: a rule's body lives in exactly one file; everywhere else holds relative-path links.
2. **No rules in tool-private directories**: project-level rule bodies live only in `AGENTS.md` and `aiDoc/`.
3. **Changes leave a trace**: behavior / architecture / contract / process / test-strategy changes are traced in the same change — decision records per `aiDoc/notes/README.md`, change plans per `aiDoc/plans/README.md`, lessons and business-requirement records per `aiDoc/memory/README.md`.
4. **Evidence and reporting discipline**: evidence is proportional to the affected surface — focused tests for logic changes, typecheck + contract tests for interface changes, real-entry smoke for user-visible output, link/format checks for doc changes; never run the full suite by reflex, never loosen filters to get green. Report results separately as passed / failed / skipped / unavailable / not-run; never describe an interrupted command as completed.
5. **Handoffs are complete**: when work is interrupted or handed over, hand off per `aiDoc/plans/handoff.TEMPLATE.md` with exact state and the next safe action.
6. **Closing double gate**: before closing out, pass the drift self-check — run the project-harness skill's `check_sync.py` when installed, otherwise verify against the `aiDoc/README.md` routing table — and fix any drift in the same change; pitfalls and recurring patterns are captured per lessons discipline. Both are part of the Definition of Done.

## Definition of Done

A change is complete only when all of the following hold:

- [ ] Code changes follow the rules in this file and the routed aiDoc child documents
- [ ] Validation commands ran per "Evidence and reporting discipline"; results reported honestly by category
- [ ] Both sides of every changed contract (producer and consumer) are traced and synchronized
- [ ] Changes needing a trace (decisions / plans / lessons) are recorded per the corresponding discipline
- [ ] Affected aiDoc documents are updated in place (facts refreshed, no appended changelog)
- [ ] Paths and symbol references in docs match the real code
- [ ] Closing double gate passed

## Doc Index and Task Routing

### Quick Reference (task family → aiDoc area)

| Task family | Read first |
|---|---|
| Module / feature / command development | `aiDoc/modules/`, `aiDoc/examples/` |
| Frontend page / feature development | `aiDoc/frontend/`, `aiDoc/examples/` |
| Contract / interface alignment | `aiDoc/contracts/boundary.md` |
| Repo structure / tech stack / workflow | `aiDoc/relations/` |
| Business requirement records | `aiDoc/memory/business/` |
| Decision records | `aiDoc/notes/` |
| Change plans and handoffs | `aiDoc/plans/` |

Coarse pointers only; the detailed file-level "task → must-read docs" routing table lives in `aiDoc/README.md`.

### Detailed Routing

The detailed "task → must-read docs" routing table and the common-entries dictionary are maintained solely in `aiDoc/README.md`. This file keeps only the quick reference, avoiding a duplicated inventory that would drift. On conflict, this file wins.

## When to Regenerate aiDoc

Regenerate or incrementally update the doc system via the generate workflow when:

- Architecture changes materially, or modules / features / commands (including frontend pages) are added or removed, leaving `aiDoc/` out of sync with the code
- `aiDoc/` child docs are added or removed, invalidating the routing table in `aiDoc/README.md` and the quick reference above; or routing tables / examples drift visibly from the real code

Small changes (a single endpoint or page) need no full regeneration — update incrementally or by area. After regeneration, verify index and path consistency.
